%[text]{"align":"center"} # PMSM Field-Oriented Control
load FOCgains.mat
%%
%[text] ## General Parameters
%[text] ![](text:image:14d3)
%[text] Nominal dc-link voltage (V) is the voltage of the electrical source, found in the block parameterization manager of your PMSM block. 
%[text] ![](text:image:13b9)
Vdc = 320;
%[text] Maximum Power (W) is the maximum machine power, found in the block parameterization manager of your PMSM block. 
MaxPower = 2638;
%[text] Maximum torque (N\*m) is the maximum machine torque, found in the block parameterization manager of your PMSM block. 
MaxTorque = 42;
%[text] Number of rotor pole pairs is the number of permanent magnet pole pairs on the rotor, found in the block parameters of your PMSM. 
%[text] ![](text:image:6737)
NPP = 20;
%[text] Inverter dc-link voltage threshold (V) is the voltage threshold to activate the power inverter, in `V`. We will set this parameter to 1V.
InvThresh = 1;
%[text] Fundamental Sample time (s) - sample time for the block. We will set it to the new solver configuration block sample time - 5e-5.
Tf = 1e-4;
%[text] Control Sample time (s) - sample time for the control system. We will set it to the new solver configuration block sample time - 5e-5.
Ts = 1e-4;
%%
%[text] ## Outer Loop Parameters
%[text] The outer loop is the speed controller loop since we are implementing velocity control.
%[text] ![](text:image:2626)
%[text] Controller Proportional Gain is the proportional gain of the speed PI controller. We calculated this in the last guided lesson and called it SpeedKp.
SpeedKp %[output:6186afa2]
%[text] Controller Integral Gain is the integral gain of the speed PI controller. We calculated this in the last guided lesson and called it SpeedKi.
SpeedKi %[output:8c5484ce]
%[text] Permanent magnet flux linkage (Wb) can be found in the block parameters of your PMSM. 
%[text] ![](text:image:1741)
flux = 0.103309826163462;
%%
%[text] ## Inner Loop Parameters
%[text] ![](text:image:4596)
%[text] D-axis current proportional gain was calculated in the last guided lesson and named DaxisKp.
DaxisKp %[output:67556cd5]
%[text] D-axis current integral gain was calculated in the last guided lesson and named DaxisKi.
DaxisKi %[output:9e906d39]
%[text] Q-axis current proportional gain was calculated in the last guided lesson and named QaxisKp.
QaxisKp %[output:44e2f0ef]
%[text] Q-axis current integral gain was calculated in the last guided lesson and named QaxisKi.
QaxisKi %[output:65e8e645]
%%
%[text] ## Timeramp and Speedramp Variables
timeramp = [0 1 2 3 4 5 6];
speedramp = [0 0.125 0.125 0.5 0.5 1 1];
%%
%[text] ## Sending Variables to the Model Workspace
%[text] Make sure to have your Simulink Model open to be able to send the variables needed for your simulation to your Model Workspace. 
%[text] Remember, variables in the Model Workspace take priority over variables in the MATLAB Workspace, so make sure to update your variables when needed, either using a similar script or by entering the Model Workspace in Simulink. 
mw9 = Simulink.data.connect("SMXQ6_600_GoKart_Project.slx");
set(mw9,"Vdc",Vdc)
set(mw9,"MaxPower",MaxPower)
set(mw9,"MaxTorque",MaxTorque)
set(mw9,"NPP",NPP)
set(mw9,"InvThresh",InvThresh)
set(mw9,"Tf",Tf)
set(mw9,"Ts",Ts)
set(mw9,"SpeedKp",SpeedKp)
set(mw9,"SpeedKi",SpeedKi)
set(mw9,"flux",flux)
set(mw9,"DaxisKp",DaxisKp)
set(mw9,"DaxisKi",DaxisKi)
set(mw9,"QaxisKp",QaxisKp)
set(mw9,"QaxisKi",QaxisKi)
set(mw9,"timeramp",timeramp)
set(mw9,"speedramp",speedramp);
%%
